//!Context Api 
-->[Generally when we are creating component there is a parent compoent and then it has child ]
-->[Suppose if you have a App.jsx it contains Dashboard.jsx and then this dashboard contain header and this header contain
    the menu component so if we need to pass a prop from App.jsx to the menu.jsx then we need to follow the hieracrchy of passing
    it to the Dashboard then it passes to the header and then header pass this as prop to menu]
//!This is called as prop drilling
-->[Even though there is no need of this data but still we are using this data and we are passing this data because any sub children is using this data]
-->[To handle this we can use redux and along with thta Context APi]

//!Creating context UserContext.js
import React from "react";
const UserContext = React.createContext()
export default UserContext

//Then we need to create a provider component <UserContext>

//!We create a compoent called UserContextProvider.jsx
import React from "react";
import UserContext from "./UserContext";
const UserContextProvider=({children})=>{
    return (
        <UserContext.Provider>
        {children}
        </UserContext.Provider>
    )
}
export default UserContextProvider;

//!After that we bind the UserContextProvider inside the fragement in the app.jsx

//*Simple way when we want to do it wihtout context api

function App() {
  const [theme, setTheme] = useState("light");
  return <Layout theme={theme} setTheme={setTheme} />;
}

function Layout({ theme, setTheme }) {
  return <Header theme={theme} setTheme={setTheme} />;
}

function Header({ theme, setTheme }) {
  return <ThemeButton theme={theme} setTheme={setTheme} />;
}

function ThemeButton({ theme, setTheme }) {
  return (
    <button onClick={() => setTheme(theme === "light" ? "dark" : "light")}>
      Switch to {theme === "light" ? "Dark" : "Light"}
    </button>
  );
}

//! this is the approach that is called as prop drilling we can also use another approach called context api where we create a context 
const [themeMode, setThemeMode] = useState("light");
  return (
    <ThemeContext.Provider value={{ themeMode,setThemeMode}}>
      {children}
    </ThemeContext.Provider>
  );
}

and then simply the component can use it 
const {  themeMode  } = useContext(ThemeContext);


//!React Redux:
It is a state management library for Javascript applications manages to share and access data across pages in a predictable way
Redux keeps one central place a store where all the data is present

Store: This is like a central wareshouse , it contains the states tree in one place
Action: This is an object that describes what to do , it consist of payload (suppose we need to add something action is add)
Dispatch: This simply sends the action to the redux (Dispatch simply updates the store using reducer)
//!reducer need to be called inside the dispatch 
Reducer:this is a pure function that takes state and action and updates  state 
selector:a function to extract data from redux store
//!createSlice and initialState are to be used in react redux   

React consist of reducers and the reducer contain properties and function 
state.todos=state.todos.filter((todo)=>todo.id!==action.payload) // this is generally used if we want to go with 
removing a todo

